k so  let's take a look at some of the  evidence that the y2k thin ideal is  coming back in i bring you the low-rise  jean yep the look made famous by celebs  like paris hilton and ex-tina dirty era  and now being rocked by some of the  biggest celebrities today i definitely  was fully committed to the low rise life  when i was in high school and i am  like cringing just thinking about  putting my body into them today like  there was nothing comfortable about the  low-rise ultra strength thong combo but  for the gen z's who did not live through  the low-rise jean era you might be  thinking but abby like it's just a type  of gene it's just a style what's the big  deal well let's take a look-see shall we  exhibit a  different dream this is me  what the actual  moving on to exhibit b seem  uncomfortable  see  not great not great at all we maybe have  come a long way since the 2000s in the  world of body neutrality and body  positivity but i worry about people  feeling the need to change their body to  fit a trend whether we're talking about  the og version or the reboot when  influencers and celebrities rock the  look it's often accompanied by an  impossibly flat tummy and jutting hip  bones and let's be real most human women  do not have flat tummies and jutted hip  bones so unlike more inclusive trends  like the high-rise mom gene this little  luke is only celebrated on a body with  no visible fat which is why the  statements made in these tick tock  videos are not far from my own thoughts  when i was a teen because not only did  the imagery in the media distort my  perception of what my body should look  like but fashion trends like this and  the people who were seen rocking said  trends further reinforced it and i am  certainly not alone with my concern as  other fellow millennials have taken to  social media to express their grievances  on what it means to have this trend make  a comeback this was me in high school  you see the bone sticking out of my  chest this 